# Erdős Problem #99 — 2026-08-30 frontier log

## Checkpoint 0: starting state

The canonical v1.7 modules still report **LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN**.  The
authoritative Target A endpoint is the source-specific response inequality

\[
\dim\ker S \ge \Delta_\Lambda-d_{\rm int},
\]

equivalently

\[
\operatorname{rank}S
\le d_{\rm int}+|C^\partial|-\Delta_\Lambda.
\]

An earlier draft described the fixed-source target as a finite unit-separated
\(Q\subseteq H\) with
\[
|Q|-|N_A(Q)|\ge \widehat p_H+1.
\]
The exact same-source capacity theorem now rules this out: every such Q has
deficiency at most \(\widehat p_H=\alpha_1(H)-|A|\).  The valid remaining source
route must add boundary/diameter control to an add-one replacement, or connect the
capacity flag directly to the Schur response inequality.  The current flag reaches,
but cannot exceed, the available same-hull slack \(\widehat p_H\).

## Checkpoint 3: fixed-source Hall-surplus route is impossible as stated

The source module's exact capacity theorem gives, for every finite unit-separated
\(Q\subseteq H\),
\[
|Q|-|N_A(Q)|\le\alpha_1(H)-|A|=\widehat p_H.
\]
The proof is the one-line replacement argument: remove the source points in
\(N_A(Q)\), insert Q, and compare the resulting unit-separated subset of H with
\(\alpha_1(H)\).  Therefore a proposed \(\widehat p_H+1\) deficiency—and the older
\(p+1\) proposal—is not an open lemma; it is incompatible with the definitions.

This audit removes a misleading branch from the frontier.  It does not change the
direct Target A endpoint
\[
\dim\ker S\ge\Delta_\Lambda-d_{\rm int},
\]
nor the first-transition and finite-gap investigations.  A source replacement can
still be useful if its complete configuration is certified to have diameter \(<D\)
(for example by placing all \(N+1\) points in \(\operatorname{int}H\)), or if it is
transported directly into the Schur response.  A same-hull Hall number alone cannot
provide the contradiction.

