## Checkpoint 1: exact finite regressions for the two local scaffold phases

The local scaffold cells have two neutral models: a degree-three monomer and a
degree-four dimer.  Both admit arbitrarily large finite patches in which the
left side is completely matchable.  This is a useful negative result: local
degree, planarity, and the neutral phase count do not by themselves imply the
left-nullity needed at the Target A endpoint.

Let


\[
 e_1=(1,0),\qquad e_2=(1/2,\sqrt3/2),\qquad
 \Lambda=\mathbb Z e_1+\mathbb Z e_2,
\]

and write
\(\|a e_1+b e_2\|^2=a^2+ab+b^2\).  For integers L >= 2, take the
parallelogram-indexed patch


\[
 H_L=\operatorname{conv}X_L,qquad
 X_L=\{c+i e_1+j e_2:0\le i,j<L\}.
\]

For the monomer phase, use c=(e_1+e_2)/3.  A lattice point
\(a e_1+b e_2\) is at distance strictly less than one from
\(x_{ij}=c+i e_1+j e_2\) exactly when


\[
 (a,b)\in\{(i,j),(i+1,j),(i,j+1)\}.
\]

Indeed, after putting r=a-i and s=b-j, the squared distance is
\(Q(1/3-r,1/3-s)\), where Q(u,v)=u^2+uv+v^2.  The three displayed
choices give Q=1/3; a direct check of the neighboring integer pairs gives
at least 1 for every other pair.  The natural finite one-layer shore


\[
 Y_L^{(3)}=\{a e_1+b e_2:0\le a,b\le L\}\setminus
 \{L e_1+L e_2\}
\]

contains all three conflicts of every x_{ij}.  The map
\(x_{ij}\mapsto i e_1+j e_2\) is a matching saturating X_L.  Thus


\[
 \nu(X_L,Y_L^{(3)})=L^2,\qquad
 |X_L|-\nu=0,qquad |Y_L^{(3)}|-\nu=2L.
\]

The omitted corner is not an arbitrary truncation: it is the only corner of
this particular monomer hull's lattice square that lies beyond the closed
unit parallel body; retaining it would not improve the left matching anyway.

For the dimer phase, take c=(e_1+e_2)/2 and


\[
 Y_L^{(4)}=\{a e_1+b e_2:0\le a,b\le L\}.
\]

The strict conflict neighborhood is exactly


\[
 N(x_{ij})=\{i e_1+j e_2,(i+1)e_1+j e_2,
 i e_1+(j+1)e_2,(i+1)e_1+(j+1)e_2\}.
\]

Here Q(\pm1/2,\pm1/2)=1/4 for the four choices, while every other
integer pair gives squared distance at least one.  Again
\(x_{ij}\mapsto i e_1+j e_2\) saturates the left side, so


\[
 \nu(X_L,Y_L^{(4)})=L^2,\qquad
 |X_L|-\nu=0,qquad |Y_L^{(4)}|-\nu=2L+1.
\]

Both patches are unit-separated translated lattice patches, and their
conflict graphs have exactly the advertised monomer or dimer local degrees.
They are not counterexamples to the Erdős problem: they deliberately contain
unit triangles and do not satisfy the source-side triangle-free hypotheses.
Their role is sharper.  Any proposed proof of


\[
 d_{\rm sc}=|X_m|-\nu(X_m,Y)\ge \Delta_\Lambda-d_{\rm int}
\]

must use a genuinely global fact tying the independent source to the chosen
smaller-optimum scaffold.  Planarity, Euler charge, local cell type, and the
neutral phase equation all survive these exact regressions while
\(d_{\rm sc}=0\) for every \(L\).

## Checkpoint 2: rectangular shear patches cannot be global minimizers

A natural triangle-free bulk ansatz is a rectangular patch of a sheared
triangular lattice: keep one unit direction and stretch the other.  This
ansatz can be excluded exactly, without assuming that a global minimizer is
lattice-based.

For \(s>1\), set

\[
 u=(1,0),\qquad v_s=s(1/2,\sqrt3/2),
\]

and, for \(r,q\ge2\), define

\[
 P_{r,q}(s)=\{i u+j v_s:0\le i<r,\ 0\le j<q\}.
\]

For an integer difference \(a u+b v_s\), its squared length is

\[
 F_s(a,b)=a^2+sab+s^2b^2
        =\left(a+\frac{s b}{2}\right)^2+\frac34s^2b^2.
\]

The patch is unit-separated.  If \(b=0\), a nonzero difference has length at
least one.  If \(|b|\ge2\), the completed-square expression is larger than
one.  For \(b=1\), the cases \(a=0\) and \(a=-1\) give
\(s^2>1\) and \(1-s+s^2=1+s(s-1)>1\).  For \(a\ge1\) the inequality is
immediate, while for \(a=-k\le-2\),
\[
 F_s(-k,1)-1=s^2-ks+k^2-1
 =\left(s-\frac{k}{2}\right)^2+\frac{3k^2}{4}-1>0.
\]
The case \(b=-1\) follows by replacing \(a\) with \(-a\).  Thus every
nonzero difference has length at least one.

The same patch has no unit equilateral triangle.  A unit equilateral
triangle would require two independent unit vectors in the difference
lattice.  The preceding calculation shows that the only unit difference
vectors are \(u\) and \(-u\): the \(b\ne0\) cases are strictly longer than
one.  Hence no three points of \(P_{r,q}(s)\) form a unit equilateral
triangle.

Nevertheless, it cannot be a global diameter minimizer for its cardinality.
Put \(A=r-1\) and \(B=q-1\).  The maximum of the convex quadratic \(F_s\) on
\([-A,A]\times[-B,B]\) occurs at a corner, and because \(sAB>0\),

\[
 \operatorname{diam}(P_{r,q}(s))^2
 =F_s(A,B)=A^2+sAB+s^2B^2.
\]

The unsheared patch \(P_{r,q}(1)\) has the same \(rq\) points and is also
unit-separated, but

\[
 F_s(A,B)-F_1(A,B)
 =(s-1)\bigl(AB+(s+1)B^2\bigr)>0.
\]

Therefore \(P_{r,q}(1)\) is a strict smaller-diameter competitor.  The
conclusion is conditional but exact:

> No global minimizer for \(D(rq)\) can be a rectangular \(s\)-sheared
> triangular-lattice patch with \(s>1\) and \(r,q\ge2\).

This removes one plausible infinite triangle-free model, but it does not
control non-rectangular shear, boundary surgery, or aperiodic configurations.
It also does not provide the missing Hall surplus.  The remaining geometric
bridge must therefore address how a genuinely irregular triangle-free
configuration differs from every regular bulk patch while preserving the
global diameter optimum.

## Checkpoint 3: a geometric cyclic circuit with only one unit of deficiency

The cyclic fundamental-circuit branch also has a small exact obstruction to
naive face-charge summation.  Let

\[
 b_k=(\cos(k\pi/3),\sin(k\pi/3)),\qquad 0\le k<6,
\]

and let \(B=\{b_0,\ldots,b_5\}\).  Define four left-shore points

\[
 \begin{aligned}
 x_1&=(-2/5,4/5),&
 x_2&=(1/20,17/10),\\
 x_3&=(2/5,3/20),&
 x_4&=(1,19/20).
 \end{aligned}
\]

Direct squared-distance checks give

\[
 \begin{aligned}
 N(x_1)=N(x_2)&=\{b_1,b_2\},\\
 N(x_3)=N(x_4)&=\{b_0,b_1\},
 \end{aligned}
\]

where \(N(x)=\{b\in B:|x-b|<1\}\).  The only borderline nonedge needed in
the calculation is \(|x_1-b_3|=1\); all other omitted cross-distances are
strictly greater than one.  The four \(x_i\) are unit-separated: the only
unit pair is \(|x_3-x_4|=1\), and the other five pairwise distances are
strictly greater than one.  The regular hexagon \(B\) is unit-separated as
well.

Consequently the induced strict-conflict graph on
\[
 C=\{x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4\},
 \qquad
 W=\{b_0,b_1,b_2\}
\]
is two copies of \(K_{2,2}\) sharing \(b_1\).  It is connected, every right
vertex has degree at least two, and

\[
 |C|=4,\qquad |W|=3,\qquad
 \nu(C,W)=3,\qquad |C|-\nu=1.
\]

Every proper left subset is matchable, so \(C\) is a minimal transversal
matroid circuit.  Its graph has \(e=8\) and \(e-2|W|=2\), hence two bounded
faces in a planar embedding, while its Hall deficiency is only one.  If a
full capacity-style shore is desired, add the three left points
\(b_3,b_4,b_5\); they are isolated cross-shore matching components and do
not alter the circuit.

This is an exact geometric cyclic regression, not merely an abstract planar
graph.  It rules out the implication

\[
 \text{“two bounded circuit faces”}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
 \text{“two independent Hall/Schur null directions.”}
\]

The cyclic branch therefore needs an overlap-sensitive linkage theorem, not
just a count of \(\kappa=e-2|W|\).  Together with the monomer and dimer
patches above, this leaves the unresolved Target A input explicitly global:
it must couple the source minimizer or the diameter boundary to the circuit
geometry.

## Checkpoint 4: activation budget and fixed-angle lattice exclusion

The canonical Target A module now contains a monotone activation theorem: starting
from any triangle-free global minimizer, strict rattlers can be moved one at a time
to regular active boundaries while preserving every old active pair and creating
exactly one new pair per step.  At most \(\pi D\) steps can terminate on the
diameter boundary, because all diameter endpoints lie on the hull and the hull
perimeter is at most \(\pi D\).  Consequently a retained strict-rattler population
of size \(\rho\) leaves at least
\[
\max\{0,\rho-5\widehat p_H-2\pi D\}
\]
unit-first activations after same-hull extension, and these activations contain a
forest of at least half that many edges.  The forest rigidity rows are independent,
so this is a constraint-rank resource rather than an automatic Schur nullity.

The canonical Target B module also now proves, by a centered fundamental-cell
estimate, that an exact subset of a unit-rhombic lattice with angle bounded away
from \(\pi/3\) and \(2\pi/3\) cannot be a sufficiently large global minimizer.  Any
one-lattice survivor must satisfy
\[
\sin\theta-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}=O(n^{-1/2}).
\]
This formalizes the earlier fixed-oblique scaling heuristic, but leaves varying-angle
and nonlattice meshes untouched.

The exact Level I endpoint remains the Target A source-response inequality and the
Target B higher-order shell-rank theorem.  The executable checks
capacity_flag.py --demo and transition_scan.py --width 2 --height 2 both pass.

## Checkpoint 5: plateau-endpoint audit (left versus right)

An attempted strengthening of the source-capacity theorem needs a correction.
If

\[
N_-:=\min\{q:D(q)=D\},
\qquad
N_+:=\max\{q:D(q)=D\},
\]

and \(A_-\) is an \(N_-\)-point subset of a \(D\)-optimal configuration,
then \(H_-:=\operatorname{conv}A_-\) has diameter \(D\), but the definition of
\(N_-\) only controls \(D(N_--1)\).  It does **not** imply
\(D(N_-+1)>D\): the plateau can continue to \(N_+\).  The only unconditional
capacity conclusion is therefore

\[
N_-
\le \alpha_1(H_-)
\le N_+.
\]

By contrast, at the right endpoint the zero-slack statement is exact.  If
\(A_+\) is any \(N_+\)-point \(D\)-optimal configuration and
\(H_+:=\operatorname{conv}A_+\), then

\[
\boxed{\alpha_1(H_+)=N_+.}
\]

The lower bound is supplied by \(A_+\).  A unit-separated set of \(N_++1\)
points in \(H_+\) would have diameter at most \(D\), forcing
\(D(N_++1)\le D\), contrary to the definition of the right endpoint and
monotonicity of \(D(\cdot)\).  Hence the rebuilt source has
\(\widehat p_{H_+}=0\), while a left-endpoint source retains at most the full
plateau slack \(N_+-N_-\).

This audit preserves the exact target: zero source slack may be used only after
right-endpoint terminalization (and a corresponding scaffold rebuild).  It must
not be silently assigned to the original source or to the left endpoint.

**LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN.  TARGET A REMAINS UNRESOLVED.**

## Checkpoint 7: facial localization of train holonomy is rejected

An attempted overlap argument assigned mod-three gain charges to bounded faces of
the train-crossing graph.  That graph is an intersection graph, not the planar
rhombic 1-skeleton: it has \(O(D)\) train vertices but \(\Theta(D^2)\) crossing
edges, and so cannot have a planar embedding for large \(D\).  The physical rhombic
complex is planar, but the gain labels live on pairs of trains rather than on its
1-skeleton edges.  Consequently the proposed facial charge, dual cut, and
overlap-safe \(O(D)\)-face conclusion are not valid.

The exact mod-three cycle lemma and its warning about edge-disjoint cycles remain
valid.  A future face-space construction must introduce a new planar incidence
object that preserves the train-pair gain; none is currently known.

## Checkpoint 8: dense low-defect cores are three-phase-balanced

The valid replacement is a graph-diameter argument.  If crossing defects satisfy
\(\sum_e\delta_e\le C_\delta D\) and the number of trains satisfies \(T\le C_TD\),
delete crossings with \(\delta_e>D^{-1+\eta}\), then iteratively delete trains of
current degree below \(\lambda D\).  The exact count is
\[
C_\delta D^{2-\eta}+C_T\lambda D^2
\]
deleted crossing edges.  Every retained component has minimum degree
\(\lambda D\), hence graph diameter at most
\(\lceil3T/(\lambda D)\rceil\).  A root-to-edge closed walk then has bounded
length and vanishing defect as \(D\to\infty\); a nonzero \(\mathbb Z/3\) gain would
force defect at least \(\pi/3\), a contradiction.  Thus each retained component
has an exact three-phase potential, with at most \(C_T/\lambda+1\) components.

For fixed \(\varepsilon>0\), choosing \(\lambda\) small leaves all but
\(\varepsilon D^2+o(D^2)\) crossing edges in only \(O_\varepsilon(1)\) balanced
components.  This is a concrete reduction of the ordered shell problem, but the
deleted interface edges are still too numerous at the boundary-rank scale to close
Target B.

## Checkpoint 6: the first-transition escape test survives at (3\times3)

The finite transition harness was rerun with the triangular-lattice comparison
shore generated in the (3\times3) rectangular window.  The window contains 14
comparison points.  Exhaustive enumeration of its triangle-free subshores gives

\[
\begin{array}{c|r}
\text{quantity}&\text{count}\\ \hline
\text{triangle-free subshores}&5339\\
\text{subshores with a first bad flag member}&5339\\
\text{first-transition triangles}&6891\\
\text{locally caged first-transition triangles}&3560\\
\text{locally escapable first-transition triangles}&3331
\end{array}
\]

Here “locally caged” means that the newly inserted point has no one-point
velocity, with every other point held fixed, that strictly increases all of its
current unit separations.  The test omits diameter constraints and uses the
floating-point coordinates generated by `transition_scan.py`; it is therefore a
reproducible diagnostic rather than an exact global theorem.  The (2\times2)
window gives the same qualitative behavior (112 caged versus 79 escapable).

The main conclusion is negative but useful: neither a deterministic circuit
ordering nor a one-point perturbation of the first inserted site can be promoted
to the missing Target A alternative.  More than half of the (3\times3) first
transition triangles are already caged in this restricted slice.  A successful
argument must either move a coupled set of sites, exploit diameter-boundary
freedom, or charge the caged angular pattern to the global Schur/source budget.

The counts are now independently reproduced by
`research/exact_lattice_transition.py`.  That checker uses integer axial
coordinates and the exact form (a^2+ab+b^2); it reports the same 5,339
nonempty triangle-free subshores, 6,891 first-transition triangles, and the
3,560/3,331 caged/escapable split.  The geometric row-window selection still
uses the stated (3\times3) dimensions, but no floating-point tolerance enters
the distance or cage classification after the points are selected.

**LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN.  TARGET A REMAINS UNRESOLVED.**

## Checkpoint 9: phase order improves the dense-core extremal count

The three-phase potential does more than certify local consistency.  Orienting
crossings cyclically between the three phase classes and using the absence of
directed 3-cycles gives the exact component bound

\[
|E(H)|\le \max_i n_i(T_H-n_i).
\]

Thus a balanced phase core has at most \((2/9+o(1))T_H^2\) crossing edges,
strictly below the generic tripartite/Mantel scale \(T_H^2/4\).  The estimate
also holds in the long-directed-cycle alternative; it is not an acyclicity
argument.  This is a genuine phase-sensitive density reduction, but it still
does not extract the complete bipartite block required by the Cartesian
determinant inequality, nor does it charge the deleted interface edges at the
boundary-rank scale.

## Checkpoint 10: exact maximal phase density is bipartite

For three equal phase classes of size (m), the phase-order bound gives
(|E|le2m^2).  Equality forces every phase triple to contain exactly two
crossings, and an explicit colouring argument then shows that the whole crossing
graph is complete bipartite (the two colour classes may split each phase class).
This identifies the exact dense endpoint, but not its near-equality stability and
not the additive two-direction train block required by the determinant estimate.

## Checkpoint 11: boundary-order density gaps are impossible for feasible sets

The tempting estimate

\[
|A|\le \frac{\pi}{2\sqrt3}D^2-\kappa D+O(1)
\]

for all triangle-free unit-separated sets of diameter at most (D) is false.  Take
the unit rhombic lattice with angle (pi/3+D^{-3}).  Its only unit lattice
vectors are the two basis directions and their negatives, so it contains no unit
equilateral triangle.  Translation-averaging the lattice in an open disk of
diameter (D) gives a set with

\[
|A|\ge \frac{\pi D^2}{4\sin(\pi/3+D^{-3})}
 =\frac{\pi}{2\sqrt3}D^2-O(D^{-1}),
\]

and the full-density count forces a unit-neighbor pair, so the minimum distance is
exactly one.  This is a rigorous family of near-optimal triangle-free feasible sets,
not a family of global minimizers.  It confirms that the remaining proof must detect
finite global optimality at the vanishing-shear scale; density and local
triangle-freeness alone cannot supply the missing boundary-order sign.

## Checkpoint 12: aligned phase wings have a quadratic diameter penalty

The exact bipartite phase endpoint supplies two opposite-side wings sharing a
phase family.  An elementary determinant identity shows that aligned wings obey

\[
D^2\ge2\bigl(|\det(U,V)|+|\det(U,W)|\bigr).
\]

At balanced equality, one wing has at least half the third phase class, so an
aligned endpoint core would force at least \((3\sqrt3/2)m^2\) diameter-square
while its \(2m^2+O(m)\) rhombi permit only \((4\sqrt3/\pi+o(1))m^2\).  The
remaining gap is explicit: the two abstract complete-bipartite wings need not yet
be shown to share a base up to a displacement parallel to their common train
direction.  The fan inequality is therefore conditional, but it converts the
phase endpoint into a concrete geometric alignment target.

The displacement audit is exact: if the two wing bases differ by (Z), the same
four-corner calculation gives

\[
D^2\ge2\left|\det(U,V-W-Z)\right|,
\qquad
D^2\ge |\det(U,V)|+|\det(U,W)|.
\]

Thus uncontrolled transverse displacement can erase the aligned factor of two;
the remaining route must control that displacement over many wing pairs or find a
second independent common direction.

## Checkpoint 13: coherent additive blocks have a (3m^2) endpoint cost

For an additive (a\times b) block whose two train directions are phase-coherent
and near the triangular angles, the block corners give

\[
D^2\ge\cos^2\!\alpha\left(a^2+b^2+2\cos(\pi/3+\beta+2\alpha)ab\right).
\]

Thus a coherent balanced (m\times m) block costs ((3-o(1))m^2) in
diameter-square.  At the exact balanced phase endpoint, a complete (m\)-by-(m)
crossing family would then contradict the global upper bound
((4\sqrt3/\pi+o(1))m^2), provided the family is an additive block.  This
strengthens the target: the unresolved step is biclique-to-additive-block
promotion (or an equivalent penalty from intervening trains), not the final
diameter comparison.

## Checkpoint 14: weighted coherent stars are conditionally excluded

The coherent-block norm estimate extends beyond balanced classes.  If a phase
star has class sizes \(a\le b\le M\), accounts for
\(R=M(a+b)+o(M^2)\) rhombi, and its \(M\times b\) pair is an additive coherent
block, then
\[
D^2\ge(1-o(1))M^2(1+t+t^2),
\qquad t=b/M,
\]
whereas \(D(n)^2<(2ct+o(1))M^2\), \(c=2\sqrt3/\pi\).  The difference
\(1+t+t^2-2ct\) is uniformly positive because its discriminant is negative.
Thus every non-degenerate full coherent-star endpoint (\(b/M\ge\tau>0\)) is
ruled out once the phase crossing family is promoted from a biclique to an
additive block.  If \(b/M\to0\), the same comparison requires the stronger
remainder \(R=M(a+b)+o(Mb)\).  The promotion remains the unresolved geometric
step.
